Credit to TPU bet, Brain bet is @JeffDean plus more! Google’s AI position is a result of early investments by Sundar Pichai in DeepMind, TPUs, cloud infrastructure, and AI products, rather than a reaction to ChatGPT.

Google's biggest strength in AI lies in its full-stack control of research, chips, cloud, products, and distribution across billions of users. The company has secured architectural control over the AI landscape by merging its custom silicon manufacturing with a unified research laboratory. Competitors pay high premiums for external chips, while Google executes complex neural calculations on its proprietary Tensor Processing Units. Building internal hardware allows engineers to scale pretraining aggressively without facing significant financial overhead.

@rohanpaul_ai: "Critics once underestimated CEO Sundar Pichai. Now, critics wonder if he’s made Google too powerful."

+1 to PSGD. The original paper by Xi-Lin is super insightful, and the follow-ups extending the preconditioner design using Lie groups are elegant.

@_arohan_: "PSGD is indeed kino. I fear not the man who has implemented 10,000 optimizers on a single problem, but I fear the man who relentlessly improved the same optimizer 10,000 times."

PSGD is a second-order stochastic optimization method that effectively handles nonconvexity and gradient noise, utilizing affine group and low-rank approximation preconditioners.

